# The guard on printing.  M1-D6 (docs/SPEC.md:865) and include/adelefeld/text.h:32-38: a printer
# of a value with a real or complex part returns NULL with length 0 when the midpoint or the
# radius of one of its real balls is not zero and has a binary exponent above
# ADF_PRINT_EXP_MAX = 100000 in absolute value.  The binary exponent is the one FLINT stores,
# x = m 2^e with 0.5 <= |m| < 1 (refs/src/flint-3.0.1/arf.rst:14-20), which is one more
# than the exponent floor(log2 |x|) of the other reading; a mag holds the same form
# (refs/src/flint-3.0.1/mag.rst:3-6).  So 2^99999 = 0.5 * 2^100000 has e = 100000, the last
# admitted exponent, and 2^100000 has e = 100001, the first refused one.  The driver holds no
# bound of its own: it answers error: LIMIT for a printer that returns NULL.
# The long operands below are the decimal expansions of 2^99999, 2^100000 and 2^51000
# (30103, 30103 and 15353 digits).  The value form has no power syntax, so a value with a
# binary exponent of 100000 needs 30103 decimal digits.
#!exit 1
# The midpoint at the last admitted exponent.  rad = 0 and 2^99999 is not a decimal, so 9.5
# goes to step 3: X(mid) = 30102, q = X - 20 + 1 = 30083, M = round(mid, 10^30083) =
# 49950104650719225397 * 10^30083 (20 significant digits, a multiple of 10^30083, so the loop
# of step 5 ends at once), R = ceil2(0.2016... * 10^30083) = 2.1e30082.
show ( ; 0)
# One more: e = 100001, above ADF_PRINT_EXP_MAX.  The printer returns NULL, the driver
# answers error: LIMIT.
show ( ; 0)
# The radius: mag holds the same form, so 2^99999 has MAG_EXP 100000 and 2^100000 has
# MAG_EXP 100001.  9.5 step 2 prints "0 +/- fmt(ceil2(rad))"; ceil2(2^99999) = 5e30102.
show (0 +/-  ; 0)
show (0 +/-  ; 0)
# The same rule in the imaginary part of a complex adele: M1-D6 speaks of "a value with a
# real or complex part", and the driver reads both coordinates.
show ((0) + ()*i ; 0)
show ((0) + ()*i ; 0)
# A value an operation produced, with both operands printable.  2^51000 has the binary
# exponent 51000 and is read exactly at prec 100000 (conventions 9.5, "Reading": a dyadic
# whose odd mantissa has at most prec bits is read exactly), so the text of 9.5 is the text
# of the exact dyadic 2^51000 with rad = 0: X = 15352, q = 15333, M = 33867166513203326645
# * 10^15333, R = 3.6e15332.  The product 2^102000 = 0.5 * 2^102001 has the binary exponent
# 102001, and at prec 100000 it is rounded, so the result is not printable.
prec 100000
show ( ; 0)
mul ( ; 0) with ( ; 0)
# prec above ADF_PRINT_EXP_MAX.  A real result rounded at prec p has a radius with the
# binary exponent -p: div (1 ; 0) with 3 at prec 100000 has MAG_EXP -100000 and at prec
# 100001 MAG_EXP -100001 (lanes/m1-repair-driver/prec_probe.c).  So above ADF_PRINT_EXP_MAX
# the driver cannot print an inexact real result of magnitude 1 or more, and it refuses the
# setting with error: LIMIT, which is a resource limit (conventions 3.1, ADF_LIMIT).
prec 100001
# prec 100000 is the last setting the driver accepts; it writes no line, and the inexact
# quotient below is printed.
prec 100000
div (1 ; 0) with 3
